::ALG2_U01_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 6, what is f(3)? {
~1 #This omits part of the expression.
=3 #Correct. 3 squared minus 4(3) plus 6 equals 9 - 12 + 6 = 3.
~6 #This is the constant term only.
~-3 #This subtracts the terms but does not add 6 correctly.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which set could represent the domain of a function shown only for inputs -2, 0, 4, and 7? {
~the set -2, 4 #This leaves out some listed inputs.
~the set 0, 7 #This leaves out -2 and 4.
~the set -7, -4, 0, 2 #These are not the listed inputs.
=the set -2, 0, 4, 7 #Correct. The domain is the set of input values.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>If g(x) = 3x - 8, what input gives g(x) = 10? {
=x = 6 #Correct. 3x - 8 = 10 gives 3x = 18, so x = 6.
~x = 2 #This gives g(2) = -2.
~x = 10 #This is the output, not the input.
~x = 18 #This forgets to divide by 3.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>What does f(5) = 12 mean? {
~When the output is 5, the input is 12. #This reverses input and output.
~The function has no input. #The input is 5.
=When the input is 5, the output is 12. #Correct. Function notation gives output for a specified input.
~The domain is only 12. #The statement identifies one input-output pair.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (4x<sup>2</sup> + 3x - 7) + (2x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 9). {
=6x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 2 #Correct. Combine like terms.
~6x<sup>2</sup> + 8x + 2 #The x terms should combine to -2x.
~2x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 16 #This subtracts instead of adding x squared terms.
~6x<sup>4</sup> - 2x + 2 #Adding like powers does not change the exponent.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (7x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 1) - (3x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 5). {
~4x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 4 #This does not distribute the subtraction to every term.
~10x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 4 #This adds instead of subtracting x squared terms.
~4x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 6 #The x term sign is incorrect.
=4x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 6 #Correct. Subtract each term in the second polynomial.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Multiply (x + 4)(x + 6). {
~x<sup>2</sup> + 10 #This omits the x term and multiplies incorrectly.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 10x + 24 #Correct. The middle terms are 4x and 6x.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 24x + 10 #This swaps the sum and product.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 10x + 24 #The signs should be positive.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Multiply (2x - 3)(x + 5). {
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 10x - 15 #This omits the -3x term.
~2x<sup>2</sup> - 7x - 15 #The middle term sign is incorrect.
=2x<sup>2</sup> + 7x - 15 #Correct. 2x squared plus 10x minus 3x minus 15.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 7x + 15 #The constant term should be negative.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression shows the greatest common factor factored from 6x<sup>3</sup> + 9x<sup>2</sup>? {
~3x(2x<sup>2</sup> + 3x) #This does not factor out the greatest possible power of x.
~6x<sup>2</sup>(x + 3) #This does not multiply back to the original expression.
~x<sup>2</sup>(6x + 9x) #The second term inside should not include x.
=3x<sup>2</sup>(2x + 3) #Correct. The GCF is 3x squared.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> + 9x + 20. {
=(x + 4)(x + 5) #Correct. 4 and 5 multiply to 20 and add to 9.
~(x + 2)(x + 10) #These factors multiply to 20 but add to 12.
~(x - 4)(x - 5) #This gives a negative middle term.
~(x + 1)(x + 20) #These factors add to 21.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> - 36. {
~(x - 18)(x + 2) #These do not multiply to x squared minus 36.
~(x - 6)(x - 6) #This expands to x squared minus 12x plus 36.
=(x - 6)(x + 6) #Correct. This is a difference of squares.
~x(x - 36) #This expands to x squared minus 36x.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>What is the complex factored form of x<sup>2</sup> + 25? {
~(x - 5)(x + 5) #This expands to x squared minus 25.
=(x - 5i)(x + 5i) #Correct. The product is x squared plus 25.
~(x + 25i)(x - 25i) #This expands to x squared plus 625.
~x(x + 25) #This expands to x squared plus 25x.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify &radic;-49. {
=7i #Correct. &radic;-49 = 7i.
~-7 #Squaring -7 gives positive 49.
~-49i #This uses the radicand instead of its square root.
~49i #The square root of 49 is 7.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What is the discriminant of x<sup>2</sup> + 4x + 13 = 0? {
~68 #This adds 4ac instead of subtracting it.
~36 #The sign should be negative.
=-36 #Correct. 4 squared minus 4(1)(13) equals 16 - 52 = -36.
~-68 #This is not b squared minus 4ac.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> + 36 = 0. {
~x = -6 and x = 6 #These solve x squared minus 36 equals 0.
~x = -36i and x = 36i #This uses 36 instead of its square root.
~x = 0 and x = 36 #These do not satisfy the equation.
=x = -6i and x = 6i #Correct. x squared equals -36.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 13 = 0. {
~x = 3 and x = -3 #These are real values and do not solve the equation.
=x = 3 - 2i and x = 3 + 2i #Correct. The quadratic formula gives 3 plus or minus 2i.
~x = -3 - 2i and x = -3 + 2i #The real part should be 3.
~x = 6 - 2i and x = 6 + 2i #The real part is not divided by 2.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> - 4x<sup>2</sup> + x + 6) by (x - 2). {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 1 #This copies coefficients instead of completing division.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 3 #The x-term sign is wrong.
=x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 3 #Correct. Synthetic division with 2 gives this quotient and remainder 0.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 3 #The constant term sign is wrong.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>When dividing by x + 4 using synthetic division, what value is used? {
=-4 #Correct. x + 4 is x - (-4).
~4 #This has the wrong sign.
~0 #This would match x.
~1 #This would match x - 1.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial is divided by x - 3 and the remainder is 0. What conclusion is correct? {
~x - 3 is not a factor. #A zero remainder means the divisor is a factor.
=x - 3 is a factor. #Correct. The divisor divides evenly.
~The quotient must be 0. #The quotient does not have to be zero.
~The polynomial must be constant. #A zero remainder can happen for many degrees.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> + x + 1) by (x + 1). {
~x<sup>2</sup> + x + 1 #This ignores the missing x squared term.
~x<sup>2</sup> - x + 2 + 1/(x + 1) #The remainder sign is wrong.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 1 #This is not the quotient.
=x<sup>2</sup> - x + 2 - 1/(x + 1) #Correct. The quotient is x squared minus x plus 2 with remainder -1.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - 9x = 0. {
~x = -3 and x = 3 #This leaves out x = 0.
=x = -3, 0, 3 #Correct. Factor x(x - 3)(x + 3).
~x = 0 and x = 9 #The quadratic factor gives plus or minus 3.
~x = -9, 0, 9 #These use the coefficient instead of the square root.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - 6x<sup>2</sup> + 11x - 6 = 0. {
=x = 1, 2, 3 #Correct. The polynomial factors as (x - 1)(x - 2)(x - 3).
~x = -1, -2, -3 #The signs are incorrect.
~x = 0, 2, 3 #Zero is not a solution.
~x = 1, 3, 6 #These are not all roots.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>4</sup> - 16 = 0 over the complex number system. {
~x = -2 and x = 2 #These are only the real solutions.
~x = -4, 4, -4i, 4i #These use 16 instead of the square root relationship.
~x = -2i and x = 2i #These are only the complex non-real solutions.
=x = -2, 2, -2i, 2i #Correct. Factor into (x - 2)(x + 2)(x squared + 4).
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Which solution has multiplicity 2 in (x + 4)(x - 1)<sup>2</sup> = 0? {
~x = -4 #This factor appears once.
~x = -1 #The factor x - 1 gives x = 1.
=x = 1 #Correct. The factor x - 1 appears twice.
~x = 4 #The factor x + 4 gives x = -4.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> + 4x + 4 = 0. {
=x = -1, -2i, 2i #Correct. Group to get (x + 1)(x squared + 4) = 0.
~x = -1, -2, 2 #The quadratic factor gives complex roots.
~x = 1, -2i, 2i #The linear factor gives -1.
~x = -4 and x = 4 #These do not solve the equation.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q26::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q26<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1; MLA.A2.AE.2<br><br>Question:<br>If p(x) = x<sup>2</sup> + 2x and q(x) = 3x - 5, what is p(x) + q(x)? {
~x<sup>2</sup> + 5x + 5 #The constant should be -5.
~x<sup>2</sup> - x - 5 #This subtracts the x terms.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 5x - 5 #Correct. Combine 2x and 3x, then include -5.
~4x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 5 #This treats 3x as x squared.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q27::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q27<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which factored form is equivalent to x<sup>2</sup> - x - 20? {
~(x - 4)(x + 5) #This gives x squared plus x minus 20.
=(x + 4)(x - 5) #Correct. 4 and -5 multiply to -20 and add to -1.
~(x - 10)(x + 2) #This gives a middle term of -8x.
~(x + 20)(x - 1) #This gives a middle term of 19x.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q28::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q28<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1; MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve (x - 2)(x<sup>2</sup> + 1) = 0. {
~x = 2 only #This leaves out complex solutions.
~x = -2, i, -i #The linear factor gives positive 2.
~x = 1 and x = -1 #These do not solve x squared plus 1 = 0.
=x = 2, i, -i #Correct. x - 2 gives 2, and x squared plus 1 gives plus or minus i.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q29::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q29<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3; MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - 4x<sup>2</sup> + x + 6 = 0, given that x = 2 is a solution. {
~x = 2 only #A cubic can have more than one solution.
~x = -3, 1, 2 #These do not match the quadratic factor.
~x = -2, 1, 3 #This set omits the known solution x = 2.
=x = -1, 2, 3 #Correct. Dividing by x - 2 gives x squared minus 2x minus 3.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q30::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q30<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to (x - 3)<sup>2</sup>? {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 9 #This omits the middle term.
=x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 9 #Correct. (x - 3)(x - 3) gives x squared minus 6x plus 9.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 9 #This is (x + 3) squared.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 3x + 9 #The middle term should be -6x.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q31::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q31<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1; MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>A graph of a polynomial shows x-intercepts at -2, 1, and 4. Which list gives the real zeros? {
=x = -2, 1, 4 #Correct. Real zeros appear at x-intercepts.
~x = 2, -1, -4 #These are opposite signs.
~x = -2i, i, 4i #Imaginary values do not appear as x-intercepts.
~x = -4, -1, 2 #These do not match the intercepts.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q32::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q32<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.5; MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation has complex non-real solutions? {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 9 = 0 #This has real solutions.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 9 = 0 #This has one repeated real solution.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 9 = 0 #Correct. This gives x squared equals -9.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 3 = 0 #This has two real solutions.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q33::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q33<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which identity checks polynomial division with dividend p(x), divisor d(x), quotient q(x), and remainder r? {
~p(x) = d(x) + q(x) + r #The divisor and quotient must be multiplied.
=p(x) = d(x)q(x) + r #Correct. This is the polynomial division identity.
~p(x) = q(x) - d(x) + r #This does not represent division.
~p(x) = r/(d(x)q(x)) #The remainder is not divided by the product.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q34::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q34<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>If a cubic polynomial is divided by a linear polynomial, what degree should the quotient usually have? {
~0 #A constant quotient would usually come from dividing linear by linear.
~1 #A linear quotient would usually come from quadratic divided by linear.
~4 #Division by a linear polynomial lowers the degree.
=2 #Correct. Degree 3 minus degree 1 gives a quotient degree of 2.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q35::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q35<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1; MLA.A2.AE.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best describes the range from ordered pairs (-1, 4), (0, 2), and (3, 4)? {
~the set -1, 0, 3 #This is the domain.
~the list 4, 2, 4 #A set does not repeat duplicate values.
=the set 2, 4 #Correct. The range is the set of output values, with duplicates listed once.
~the set -1, 0, 2, 3, 4 #This combines inputs and outputs.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q36::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q36<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>What is the first step that preserves all solutions when solving x<sup>3</sup> - 16x = 0? {
=Factor out x #Correct. Factoring gives x(x squared - 16) = 0.
~Divide both sides by x #This can lose x = 0.
~Take the cube root of 16 #The equation is not x cubed equals 16.
~Add 16 to both sides #This does not isolate or factor correctly.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q37::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q37<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to 2x(x - 5) + 3(x - 5)? {
~(2x + 3)(x + 5) #The common factor is x - 5, not x + 5.
~(2x - 3)(x - 5) #The sign of 3 is incorrect.
=(2x + 3)(x - 5) #Correct. Factor out the common binomial x - 5.
~5x(x - 5) #This incorrectly combines 2x and 3 as 5x.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q38::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q38<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.5; MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve (x + 3)(x<sup>2</sup> - 4) = 0. {
=x = -3, -2, 2 #Correct. Solve each factor equal to 0.
~x = 3, -2, 2 #The factor x + 3 gives x = -3.
~x = -3, -4, 4 #The quadratic factor gives plus or minus 2.
~x = -2 and x = 2 #This leaves out x = -3.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q39::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q39<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A rectangle has width x, length x + 6, and area 91. What are the reasonable dimensions? {
~width 13, length 19 #This uses the rejected factor value incorrectly.
~width -13, length -7 #Negative dimensions are not reasonable.
~width 91, length 97 #This uses the area as the width.
=width 7, length 13 #Correct. x(x + 6) = 91 gives x = 7 or -13, and the reasonable dimensions are 7 and 13.
}

::ALG2_U01_UA_Q40::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_UA_Q40<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which response best completes a modeling solution? {
~Only list the algebraic answer with no units. #A modeling answer needs interpretation.
=State the solution, units, and whether it is reasonable in context. #Correct. Modeling requires solving and interpreting.
~Reject every negative number in every context. #Some contexts can allow negative values.
~Ignore restrictions after solving. #Restrictions are part of interpretation.
}
